    <!-- ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
         Material3 color roles (audit EG, 2026-07-22)

         `Base.Theme.EgyptianWriter` (Theme.Material3.DayNight) never defined
         colorSurface/colorOnSurface/colorSurfaceVariant/colorOnSurfaceVariant/
         colorOutline in EITHER values/themes.xml or values-night/themes.xml
         (verified: neither file mentions any of the 5). Any widget that
         doesn't explicitly override its own color — e.g. MaterialCardView's
         cardBackgroundColor, unset in item_symbol_card.xml — silently fell
         through to Material3's own generic baseline default for whichever
         mode was active: a barely-visible near-white in light mode (easy to
         miss in casual testing) and near-black in dark mode (the reported
         bug: Bliss symbol cards rendering as solid black squares).

         These 5 names are defined here (light values) and given matching
         DARK values in values-night/colors.xml — see that file's own header
         for why only these 5 got a real dark redesign, and not the
         pre-existing d_* set (which has been present, but literally
         identical to its l_* counterpart — i.e. non-functional — since
         before this audit; completing it is a larger, separate, higher-risk
         effort spanning many already-shipped screens this change does not
         touch).

         Scope: item_symbol_card.xml's MaterialCardView chrome (background,
         border, position/gloss/source-word/badge text) is the only consumer
         of these roles right now. NOT used for the Bliss symbol's own
         *rendering surface* — see bliss_symbol_canvas below for why that
         needs to stay theme-independent regardless of how well-designed a
         dark colorSurface is.
    ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
    <color name="m3_colorSurface">#ede7de</color>
    <color name="m3_colorOnSurface">#402a1f</color>
    <color name="m3_colorSurfaceVariant">#dbcfbd</color>
    <color name="m3_colorOnSurfaceVariant">#281e17</color>
    <color name="m3_colorOutline">#bd9e80</color>

    <!--
        Fixed, theme-independent background for any surface that directly
        renders a Bliss BCI-AV SVG symbol (card_symbol_svg_image, MIXED's SVG
        container, CHIPS' chip icon slot).

        ## Why this does NOT vary with day/night, unlike m3_colorSurface above
        Every bundled Bliss SVG (assets/bliss/svg/*.svg) hardcodes its line
        art as `stroke: rgb(0,0,0)` — pure black, baked into the asset file,
        with no theme awareness of its own (verified across sampled assets).
        If this surface followed the (correctly dark, in dark mode)
        m3_colorSurface above, the fixed-black strokes would render
        invisible against it — reproducing the exact bug this whole change
        set out to fix, just via a different, "more correct-looking" cause.
        A real fix needs one of: (a) dynamically re-tinting every SVG
        drawable per theme at render time, or (b) keeping the rendering
        surface itself always light, since the asset's color can't adapt on
        its own. (b) is what CHIPS view already does (see
        item_bliss_chip.xml's own header comment) and is the simpler,
        lower-risk, already-proven choice — applied consistently to CARDS
        and MIXED here too, alongside an explicit dark tint on the drawable
        itself (bliss_symbol_tint below) for defence-in-depth.
    -->
    <color name="bliss_symbol_canvas">#dbcfbd</color>
    <!-- Explicit dark tint applied to every Bliss SVG drawable, regardless
         of theme — reuses bliss_chip_text so CARDS/MIXED match CHIPS'
         already-established, contrast-verified tint exactly (8.55:1 on
         bliss_symbol_canvas; the asset's own native rgb(0,0,0) already
         provides 13.67:1, so this tint is a defensive normalisation, not a
         contrast fix in itself — see BlissSymbolCardAdapter/MixedBlissRowView
         for why explicit tinting is still worth doing regardless). -->
    <color name="bliss_symbol_tint">@color/bliss_chip_text</color>

</resources>
